# Log tick marks: annotation_logticks

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`annotation_logticks()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/annotation_logticks.html), in
the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An
example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the
page is also a list of what remains.

`annotation_logticks()` draws tick marks for a log scale inside the panel, closer together towards
each power of the base: long ones at the powers, middle ones at the fives and short ones between, so
they suit base 10 best. It is superseded by `guide_axis_logticks()`. It expects data already on a
log scale, through `scale_x_log10()` and `scale_y_log10()` or by taking logs before plotting; under
`coord_transform()`, which takes the logs itself, it needs `scaled = FALSE`. Giggly has log scales,
`Giggly.Plot.scale_x_log10/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_y_log10/2`, but no `annotation_logticks()`,
since ggplot2 has superseded it, and its replacement draws a different plot: `guide_axis_logticks()`
draws the ticks on the axis, outside the panel, where `annotation_logticks()` draws them inside. So
no example here is drawn, and none names what it waits for.

## Log ticks on chosen sides

```r
# Make a log-log plot (without log ticks)
a <- ggplot(msleep, aes(bodywt, brainwt)) +
 geom_point(na.rm = TRUE) +
 scale_x_log10(
   breaks = scales::trans_breaks("log10", \(x) 10^x),
   labels = scales::trans_format("log10", scales::math_format(10^.x))
 ) +
 scale_y_log10(
   breaks = scales::trans_breaks("log10", \(x) 10^x),
   labels = scales::trans_format("log10", scales::math_format(10^.x))
 ) +
 theme_bw()

a + annotation_logticks()                # Default: log ticks on bottom and left
a + annotation_logticks(sides = "lr")    # Log ticks for y, on left and right
a + annotation_logticks(sides = "trbl")  # All four sides
```

ggplot2's `msleep`, which Giggly has as `Giggly.Datasets.msleep/0`, describes the sleep of
mammals. Some lack a brain weight, and `na.rm = TRUE` drops them without a warning, as Giggly drops
any row missing a position.

Not drawn: Giggly has no `annotation_logticks()`, as the About says.

## Ticks outside the panel

```r
a + annotation_logticks(sides = "lr", outside = TRUE) +
 coord_cartesian(clip = "off")  # Ticks outside plot
```

Not drawn: Giggly has no `annotation_logticks()`, as the About says.

## Without the minor grid lines

```r
# Hide the minor grid lines because they don't align with the ticks
a + annotation_logticks(sides = "trbl") + theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
```

Not drawn: Giggly has no `annotation_logticks()`, as the About says.

## Taking logs first

```r
# Another way to get the same results as 'a' above: log-transform the data before
# plotting it. Also hide the minor grid lines.
b <- ggplot(msleep, aes(log10(bodywt), log10(brainwt))) +
 geom_point(na.rm = TRUE) +
 scale_x_continuous(name = "body", labels = scales::label_math(10^.x)) +
 scale_y_continuous(name = "brain", labels = scales::label_math(10^.x)) +
 theme_bw() + theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank())

b + annotation_logticks()
```

Not drawn: Giggly has no `annotation_logticks()`, as the About says.

## Log coordinates

```r
# Using a coordinate transform requires scaled = FALSE
t <- ggplot(msleep, aes(bodywt, brainwt)) +
  geom_point() +
  coord_transform(x = "log10", y = "log10") +
  theme_bw()
t + annotation_logticks(scaled = FALSE)
```

Not drawn: Giggly has no `annotation_logticks()`, as the About says.

## Tick lengths

```r
# Change the length of the ticks
a + annotation_logticks(
  short = unit(.5,"mm"),
  mid = unit(3,"mm"),
  long = unit(4,"mm")
)
```

Not drawn: Giggly has no `annotation_logticks()`, as the About says.